WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.435 --> 00:00:03.102
(clock ticking)

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.230
- There's this one lake
in Central America that,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.800
if you look closely enough,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.400
you're gonna see the tops
of trees in the lake.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.480
And these, these are not islands.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.833
These are hilltops.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.690
This all used to be a
dense jungle, not a lake.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.680
But then they built dams and
all sorts of infrastructure

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.300
to flood this forest, all
in the name of solving

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.687
a centuries-old puzzle, which was,

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:33.507
"How do we make it faster and easier

00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:36.177
"to travel between here and here,

00:00:36.177 --> 00:00:37.327
"even in spite of the fact

00:00:37.327 --> 00:00:39.507
"that you have two massive continents

00:00:39.507 --> 00:00:41.350
"blocking any waterway?"

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.470
But there was hope,

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.840
if you look down here at this
one thin stretch of land.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.460
Here it's only 80 kilometers across,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.680
and it looked so easy to
just cut a waterway into it.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:53.513
No.

00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:56.327
Turns out, that puzzle was not easy.

00:00:56.327 --> 00:00:58.560
(suspenseful music)

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.390
This little 80 kilometer strip of land

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.010
proved to be really hard to cut through,

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.950
but as long as international
trade continued to grow,

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.530
there continued to be great
powers looking for new waterways

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.670
to shorten their routes and
to increase their power,

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.610
for whoever controls
the water has the power.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.500
And these great powers always focused

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.210
on this narrow stretch of land,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.240
from the Spanish in the 14th century

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.940
to the Scottish in the 15th.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.440
But then, the most earnest and
serious attempt was in 1880,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.690
when this guy, a French
engineer, Ferdinand de Lesseps,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.310
shows up and is like, "Hey, I got this."

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.610
He had just completed

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:38.330
another big world-changing
canal over here.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.497
He was feeling very
confident, and he's like,

00:01:40.497 --> 00:01:41.577
"Yup, I'm your guy.

00:01:41.577 --> 00:01:42.847
"Let's build another canal.

00:01:42.847 --> 00:01:43.680
"It'll be easy.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.807
"We'll just dig down to sea
level and re-route the river,

00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:49.307
"like we did in Egypt with the Suez Canal

00:01:49.307 --> 00:01:51.980
"and it'll be great and I'll be famous."

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.350
But listen, de Lesseps,
Egypt is a flat desert

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.340
and Panama is not.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.540
These mountains that you wanna dig through

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.160
are well above sea level.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.990
So if you wanna dig down to
sea level to make a flat canal,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.340
it's not gonna be easy.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.220
Meanwhile, another French
engineer comes to de Lesseps

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.737
and he's like, "Dude,
this isn't gonna work.

00:02:09.737 --> 00:02:11.197
"It's way too much digging.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.947
"Instead of bringing the
mountains down to sea level,

00:02:13.947 --> 00:02:17.460
"what you need to do is bring
the sea up to the mountains."

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.420
And in a moment of
collaborative creativity,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.350
de Lesseps admits that he was wrong

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.160
and begins construction
based on the new plans,

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.460
and then he promotes this guy
as the head of engineering

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.940
and says, "Great idea."

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.267
No, de Lesseps was like,

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:33.237
"Bring the ocean up to the mountains?

00:02:33.237 --> 00:02:34.387
"That sounds stupid.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:35.247
"Screw you.

00:02:35.247 --> 00:02:36.457
"I'm the famous one.

00:02:36.457 --> 00:02:38.427
"The canal is going to be at sea level,

00:02:38.427 --> 00:02:40.150
"just like we did with the Suez Canal."

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.180
So the French start digging.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.510
Well, actually it wasn't the French.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:45.640
It was a ton of poorly
paid migrant workers

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:46.820
who were doing the digging,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.020
hacking away at the jungle with machetes

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.560
and then digging by hand with
pickaxes in sweltering heat,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.370
knee deep in sitting water,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.380
which is heaven for, you guessed it,

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.610
disease infested mosquitoes,
which is terrible news.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.020
But luckily, de Lesseps the engineer

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.730
was always there in the
trenches with his workers,

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.400
wielding a pickax alongside his people.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.710
Yeah, no, of course he wasn't.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.140
He was just hanging out in France,

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.340
only visiting Panama twice
over the course of seven years

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.100
to just check in on the project.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.560
And what he realized when he showed up

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.870
seven years into this digging

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.220
is that things were going horribly.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.510
People were dying, 40 or 50 a day.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.084
All in all, 20 thousand workers died

00:03:26.084 --> 00:03:27.980
trying to dig this canal.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.750
Thousands died from disease and accidents,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.140
and we will never know their names.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.610
And it's not just that people are dying,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.090
it's that the digging
isn't working either.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.520
They would do a bunch of digging one day,

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.780
and then the next day,
the rain would bring in

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.070
all of the mud that they
just dug back into the canal.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.640
So de Lesseps shows up,
realizes this is happening

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.777
and is like, "This is not working.

00:03:47.777 --> 00:03:50.460
"Is there another way to dig this canal?"

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.997
And then he has a flashback
to seven years earlier.

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:55.400
"Bring the sea up to the mountains."

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.233
And he's like,

00:03:56.233 --> 00:03:57.827
"Oh yeah, that was actually
a really good idea.

00:03:57.827 --> 00:04:01.210
"My bad, we actually are gonna
do that other plan instead."

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.450
But no, it was too late.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.770
The investors were done.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.470
They had lost way too much money.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:06.870
20 thousand people were dead,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.150
malaria and yellow fever were rampant.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.520
This was a fatal project.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.530
So the company that owned all of this work

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.100
had to abandon it and leave
it unfinished in 1889.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.730
Everyone gave up and left,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.450
except for one of the top
engineers on the project,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.283
this guy.

00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:23.570
I'm just gonna call him Philippe,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.690
'cause after researching
him for many days,

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.780
I now feel like I'm on a
first name basis with him.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.300
And by the way, he becomes
a giant part of the story,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.650
starting now.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.350
So Philippe was this French
engineer and businessman,

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.410
and he was obsessed with the canal.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.090
He also had a huge financial
stake in the project,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.507
and where some investors would say,

00:04:40.507 --> 00:04:44.260
"Darn, too bad, we invested
in a bad bet, let's move on."

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.447
Philippe was like, "No, I
will do whatever it takes

00:04:47.447 --> 00:04:49.500
"to make sure that this is not a failure."

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.090
And what happens next is one
of the most insane things

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.288
I have ever researched in my
entire life, so buckle up.

00:04:55.288 --> 00:04:56.220
(intriguing music)

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.314
- [Newscaster] The winds of
change is blowing up a gale

00:04:58.314 --> 00:05:00.510
in the Panama Canal zone.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.030
- Okay, so let's get some
context really quick.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:03.930
It's 1900 at this point.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.160
The US is really starting to get a handle

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.650
on this whole Imperialism
thing that they've been doing.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.520
They just fabricated a war with Spain,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.230
which led them to conquer the Philippines,

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.690
Guam and Puerto Rico.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.060
They'd just overthrown
Hawaii's government.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.870
They annexed Wake Island
and American Samoa.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.027
They're like, "Dude, this
overseas empire thing

00:05:21.027 --> 00:05:23.080
"is kinda working out for us."

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:26.050
The President at the
time was Teddy Roosevelt,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.090
who we've seen in previous episodes,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:29.800
and Teddy is looking around this region

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.120
that the US is increasingly taking over,

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.210
and he sees an opportunity
to dramatically increase

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.380
the naval power of the United States,

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.727
if they could just unite the
Atlantic and the Pacific.

00:05:40.727 --> 00:05:42.680
"It's time to build a canal", says Teddy.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.930
So of course they looked
somewhere in Central America,

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.200
and the original plan that
the US wanted to pursue

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.310
was to build a canal not in Panama,

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.980
but here in Nicaragua.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:54.010
It wasn't the shortest
stretch of land in this area,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.270
but there was a huge lake here,

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.760
which would cut down on the digging,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.040
and it just made a lot of sense.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.190
So they started doing
surveys and making plans

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.200
to build a canal in Nicaragua.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.830
Meanwhile, Philippe the
Frenchman is still down here

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.450
at his failed project,

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.860
and he's seen the US become
interested in the canal,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.800
and he's like, "This is
my moment of vindication."

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.797
So he goes to the US
Congress and he's like,

00:06:14.797 --> 00:06:17.357
"Hey guys, I know you're
looking at Nicaragua,

00:06:17.357 --> 00:06:18.887
"you've been doing all this site planning

00:06:18.887 --> 00:06:21.087
"and surveys and stuff, but guess what?

00:06:21.087 --> 00:06:24.097
"Right next to this proposed
site for your canal,

00:06:24.097 --> 00:06:25.997
"there are volcanoes.

00:06:25.997 --> 00:06:28.557
"Big, scary, belching volcanoes.

00:06:28.557 --> 00:06:31.227
"And one of them just erupted around here,

00:06:31.227 --> 00:06:33.280
"sort of next to your site."

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.300
Nicaragua even had a
postage stamp that featured

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.970
one of these nearby volcanoes erupting.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.730
So the Frenchman goes
around to the US Senators

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.417
and distributes this
postage stamp and says,

00:06:44.417 --> 00:06:47.067
"Look, scary volcanoes in Nicaragua.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:48.627
"Don't build your canal here

00:06:48.627 --> 00:06:51.800
"or they'll be destroyed
by lava or something."

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:52.710
And guess what?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.090
It totally worked.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:58.150
The US Senate got cold feet
about their Nicaragua canal plan

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.917
and they're like, "Uh, we're
not gonna do this anymore.

00:07:00.917 --> 00:07:02.360
"Let's find another place to build it."

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.287
And Philippe is like,

00:07:03.287 --> 00:07:05.257
"Oh my gosh, I can't
believe that just worked.

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:06.840
"Stamps with volcanoes?"

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.977
He's all giddy, and he's like,

00:07:07.977 --> 00:07:10.727
"Hmm, I wonder where there
would be a good place

00:07:10.727 --> 00:07:12.127
"to build a canal.

00:07:12.127 --> 00:07:14.217
"Oh, wait a minute, why not Panama?

00:07:14.217 --> 00:07:17.127
"We the French already did a
ton of work to get you started.

00:07:17.127 --> 00:07:19.257
"Why don't you just pick
up where we left off?

00:07:19.257 --> 00:07:22.067
"We'll sell you all of our
gear, all of our stuff.

00:07:22.067 --> 00:07:23.100
"It'll be a great deal."

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.780
And the US is like, "But isn't
Panama a part of Colombia?"

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.420
And yes indeed, at the time,

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.490
Panama was actually a part of Colombia.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:31.740
It was not an independent country.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.077
So the United States Congress is like,

00:07:33.077 --> 00:07:34.157
"Okay, listen Philippe.

00:07:34.157 --> 00:07:36.877
"We will buy you out of your canal project

00:07:36.877 --> 00:07:38.077
"that you clearly failed on,

00:07:38.077 --> 00:07:40.417
"but we will only do it
if we can get approval

00:07:40.417 --> 00:07:43.170
"from Colombia, who
literally owns this land."

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.487
And Philippe is secretly like,

00:07:44.487 --> 00:07:48.037
"Uh, there's no way that
Colombia is going to go for this.

00:07:48.037 --> 00:07:49.367
"They're gonna reject the plan

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:51.127
"and then the US is gonna back out

00:07:51.127 --> 00:07:52.607
"and I'm gonna lose all my money

00:07:52.607 --> 00:07:54.120
"and I have to make this happen."

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.140
Philippe was very
serious about this canal.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.980
So this is where Philippe
starts to get really conniving

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.340
to pull out all the stops
to get the canal built.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.710
So he goes to the people who live

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.557
in the region of Colombia
called Panama, and he says,

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:08.517
"Hey guys, I know you've
sorta been wanting

00:08:08.517 --> 00:08:11.517
"to be independent from
Colombia for a long time.

00:08:11.517 --> 00:08:13.597
"Bogota is so far away,
they don't care about you.

00:08:13.597 --> 00:08:14.957
"You could be your own country.

00:08:14.957 --> 00:08:18.167
"What if I, a French
engineer, could guarantee

00:08:18.167 --> 00:08:20.637
"your independence without a bloody war?

00:08:20.637 --> 00:08:22.247
"Just leave the details to me.

00:08:22.247 --> 00:08:25.350
"Make me your ambassador and
I will make this happen."

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.740
And the Panamanians are like,
"But dude, you're French."

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.667
And he's like, "Yeah yeah
yeah, but you're Colombian,

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:33.047
"so why don't we team up in
the name of creating Panama,

00:08:33.047 --> 00:08:34.010
"a new country?"

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.477
And they were like, "Okay fine, Philippe.

00:08:35.477 --> 00:08:37.557
"You're our representative,
our ambassador now.

00:08:37.557 --> 00:08:38.627
"Just give us the signal

00:08:38.627 --> 00:08:40.527
"when we should rise up
for our independence,

00:08:40.527 --> 00:08:41.727
"but you promise to make sure

00:08:41.727 --> 00:08:44.820
"that Colombia's not gonna come
in and quell our uprising."

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.267
And Philippe's like, "Yeah yeah
yeah, I'll take care of it."

